---
name: klic-design
description: Use when designing, implementing, reviewing, or adapting an interface with KLIC; apply current KLIC design rules and retrieve matching components, tokens, themes, and delivery guidance.
---

# KLIC Design

Use the KLIC contract as source of truth. Read `klic_get_contract` via MCP or, when its CLI is published, use `bunx --bun --package=klic-design@<published-version> klic contract`. Record `release`, `designSystem.revision`, actual `responder`, and toolkit versions together. Refresh when any identifier changes. Do not rely on remembered counts, versions, package status, or URLs.

## Select and apply KLIC references

- Search with `klic_search` filters, follow `next`, then inspect candidates with `klic_get_item`. Use `brief` or `compact` to discover and `full` to implement; record the detail level.
- For charts, consult `design.chart`; show exact values beside synthetic samples, match labels/units, and follow accessibility and QA limits.
- Reuse charts through `klic-design/charts`; its optional React view is `klic-design/charts/react`. Send rows only via local stdio MCP; HTTP and WebMCP expose metadata.
- `ready` means available; `planned` means proposed. Check `use`, `avoid`, `states`, `recovery`, token mappings, limitations, source notices, and the design contract. `not-specified` means evidence is missing, not that support exists.
- Check responsibilities, preset bounds, adoption policy, conflict order, scope, and completion checks before changing behavior; adopt selectively.
- Keep token paths under `klic` and preserve Primitive → Brand → Semantic → Component. Use KLIC blue for action, selection, and progress; navy text and neutral surfaces; amber warnings and red danger. Success uses blue, a check icon, and clear wording. Do not introduce green, mint, sage, teal, or green-tinted neutrals.
- Use only catalog-listed fonts and preserve their notices. Preserve semantic HTML, keyboard operation, visible focus, responsive behavior, and explicit loading, empty, filtered, and error states. A demo or checklist is not accessibility certification.

## Package and motion

`klic-design` provides the skill, token/theme SDK, fonts, CLI, and `components/*`, `blocks/*`, `motions/*` subpaths. Components preserve native HTML references; blocks are React demos; motion recipes include CSS/React examples, not always precompiled components. Search motions with `klic_search_motions` and inspect `klic_get_motion` before adoption.

## Deliver source safely

Read `klic_get_install_plan` and raw source only when implementation or source use is in scope. Review its manifest, scope, commands, and notices; run `bunx --bun --package=klic-design@latest klic add <kind> <id> --out <new-folder> --dry-run` first. Preserve source bytes and notices. Source returns raw code only. Create files only when requested, in a new output folder.

Check the live contract's `integrations.skill.npmPackage` before giving install commands. If `published`, use `bun add <name>` or `npm install <name>`; optional `skills experimental_sync` is experimental. If `source-ready`, use the current local tarball or canonical skill file; a tarball is not publication evidence. Use `bunx --bun` only for a CLI confirmed published. MCP and API are read-only. WebMCP is feature-detected and scoped to the open tab. Figma delivery is a manifest and migration guide; live sync is unavailable.

## Report evidence

Separate KLIC facts from inferences. Tie claims to returned fields and checks actually run; mark unchecked behavior unverified. Checklists and source links alone do not prove product behavior.
